WEBVTT

00:02.400 --> 00:05.294
Figure three. A place to work.

00:07.800 --> 00:21.380
Agent work has to happen somewhere on disk. In Keelson, that's a project: a name and a root path. Chats, workflow runs, and a notebook all attach to it, so you bind work to a folder once.

00:22.580 --> 00:25.346
Register a repo, and that's the whole record.

00:27.680 --> 00:35.804
Start a chat inside the repo, and it binds to that project on its own. Ask about the tests, and tell it a decision you've made.

00:37.980 --> 00:46.296
The agent works right in the project root. It reads the real files, then writes the test command and your decision into the project notebook.

00:48.880 --> 01:03.756
Here's how the notebook works. It's one markdown document per project, and before every chat turn there, Keelson puts it in front of the agent. The agent adds to it with a note tool, and you can edit it too. A conversation ends. The notebook stays.

01:04.606 --> 01:12.090
In the browser, it's right there under Memory. Plain markdown, and it lives in Keelson, not in your repo.

01:13.446 --> 01:19.714
Now a brand new chat in the same repo. No history, and nothing read yet. Just ask.

01:20.746 --> 01:27.462
It already knows the test command and the UTC rule, because the notebook came in with its very first turn.

01:28.186 --> 01:48.230
Why work this way? The agent works in place, on your real checkout, so chat edits land in your working tree as if you made them. Workflow runs can isolate in a worktree instead, and that comes later. And the context you build up belongs to the project, not to one conversation.

01:48.830 --> 01:53.148
One name, one root, one notebook. A place to work.

01:55.848 --> 01:59.572
Keelson. Lay the keel, raise the ribs.
